WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.452
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.452 --> 00:00:02.910
GABE WEISS: Hi, In
this video, I'll

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.200
introduce you to Datastream.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.750
Then I'll walk through setting
up a stream from Oracle

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.490
to Cloud Storage.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.730
I'm Gabe Weiss, a developer
advocate for Google Cloud.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.180
Datastream is a serverless
Change Data Capture, or CDC,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.830
and replication service.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:18.630
Datastream makes it
easy to synchronize

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.330
data across heterogeneous
databases and applications

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.090
reliably and with
minimal latency.

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.940
Datastream provides a
simple, integrated experience

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.243
to guide you through
every step of the process.

00:00:29.243 --> 00:00:30.660
It's serverless,
so you don't need

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:32.970
to worry about managing
or monitoring instances.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.040
And continuous data
replication means

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.500
you can unify data
across multiple sources

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.400
and applications and make
it accessible in real time.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.930
It helps power a wide
range of use cases,

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.610
including data replication for
analytics, database migrations,

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.550
event driven architectures,
AI and machine learning, data

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.420
lakes, and many more.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.690
Whether your source
database is on premises,

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.630
self-hosted in Google Cloud,
or running on another Cloud,

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.220
Datastream helps
you quickly unlock

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.770
the benefits of real time CDC.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.110
Now let's walk through
the Datastream experience.

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.360
You can easily find
Datastream by searching for it

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:09.240
in the Search box at the top
of the Cloud platform console.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.000
You can also find it from the
Google Cloud console navigation

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.250
under Big Data.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.470
To get started, you'll
create a stream.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.570
This represents the
end-to-end process

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:20.580
of streaming changes
from your source database

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.860
to your destination.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.920
First, let's define some basic
information about the stream.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.050
I want to ingest data
from an Oracle database

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.461
I have running on GCE to a
Cloud Storage destination.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.650
Once I've defined my source
and destination types,

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.840
Datastream shows me the
configuration required

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.490
for successful streaming.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.100
Datastream is unique in that
it reflects all of the source

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.930
and destination
configuration requirements

00:01:44.930 --> 00:01:46.610
directly within the
creation process

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.360
to take out the
guesswork involved.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.972
For example, since I
chose Oracle as my source,

00:01:51.972 --> 00:01:53.680
I can see the requirements
for setting up

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.450
the necessary login
configurations

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.240
and permissions on Oracle.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.490
After confirming the
necessary prerequisites,

00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:03.410
I'm ready to define my source.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.987
I can do this by creating a
connection profile, a resource

00:02:06.987 --> 00:02:08.570
that represents the
information needed

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.070
to connect to a database.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.830
These profiles aren't locked
to an individual stream.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.350
This means I can reuse
profiles if I want to,

00:02:15.350 --> 00:02:17.540
and also allows me to hand
off creating the source

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.790
configuration to someone
else in my organization

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:22.590
who's in charge of
connecting to the database.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.320
I've already created
a connection profile

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.990
for an Oracle database
running on Google Cloud's VM,

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.760
so I'll select it.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.190
Whether I create my source
connection profile now

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.780
or use the one I
create in advance,

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.760
I can test connectivity
to make sure the data

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.530
stream is able to reach it.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:39.170
Datastream supports both
public and private connectivity

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:41.730
methods to easily and
securely connect to sources,

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.820
whether they're hosted,
be it on Google Cloud,

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:47.600
on premise, or on
other Cloud providers.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.340
Once the connectivity
test passes,

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.053
I can continue to configure
the stream for my source.

00:02:52.053 --> 00:02:54.470
Next, I want to select the
objects that this stream should

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:55.730
pull from my source.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:02:57.680
Using the selection
UI, can define

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.830
which schemas and tables to
stream from my Oracle source.

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:03.530
I will select the entire HR
schema and a couple of tables

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.200
from the movie schema.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.940
For the objects I
selected, I also

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.380
need to bring over
the historical data.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.880
So I leave the backfill
historical data enabled.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.850
Now I'm ready to define the
destination for my stream.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.280
Here again, I can create
a new connection profile

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.010
for my destination or
use an existing one.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.930
I'll select the GCS
bucket I have ready to go.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.840
Now, Datastream allows me to
specify a path within my Cloud

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.312
Storage bucket where the
streamed data will be stored.

00:03:31.312 --> 00:03:33.520
For this demo, I want files
to be written to the root

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.830
folder of the bucket.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:38.920
Datastream allows me to find the
right balance between file size

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.540
and data freshness that's
right for my use case.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:44.000
It's important for me to have
my data available quickly,

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.810
so I'll reduce the writing delay
to destination to 15 seconds.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.170
This means the files written
to GCS will be smaller,

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:52.800
but the data will
be available faster.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.670
Well, that was easy.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.500
I've connected and
configured my stream.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.810
All that's left is to
validate the end-to-end stream

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:01.480
configuration and
start moving data.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.330
Validation passed successfully.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.230
I can trust that my
stream will run smoothly.

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.835
I'll create and start the
stream to run it immediately.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.190
Once my stream is created,
I can monitor its progress

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.070
and see if it
encounters any issues.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.079
Datastream will perform
the historical backfill

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:21.463
and continuously replicate
changes as they happen.

00:04:21.463 --> 00:04:23.380
I can monitor different
metrics of the stream,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.990
such as throughput,
latencies, and more.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:27.745
That's it.

00:04:27.745 --> 00:04:29.120
I've quickly and
easily shown you

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.090
how to continuously ingest
changes from an Oracle database

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.052
into Cloud Storage
using Datastream.

00:04:34.052 --> 00:04:35.510
And since Datastream
is serverless,

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.390
it'll automatically scale up and
down to handle my data volume

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:40.430
and maintain minimal latency.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.800
With change data now
continuously streaming

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:45.410
into Cloud Storage, I can
easily leverage products

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.090
like Dataflow to process
the data as needed.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:49.940
For example, I
could use a purpose

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.730
built template to replicate
the data into BigQuery or Cloud

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.500
SQL for Postgres.

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:57.380
Datastream is the fast,
easy, and reliable way

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.120
to seamlessly
deliver change data

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.250
streams from your
database to Google Cloud

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:04.520
to support Analytics, database
replication, and event driven

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:05.420
architectures.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.520
It's serverless, high
performance, and easy to use.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:11.310
Now, it's your turn
to give it a try.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.970
You can get started from the
Google Cloud console or learn

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.970
more at
cloud.google.com/datastream.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.910
Thanks for watching.

